WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:04.090
SPEAKER: Cloud SQL Insights
is a simple open tool

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:06.910
that helps developers quickly
understand and resolve

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.850
database performance
issues on Cloud SQL.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.280
It provides developers
with a single interface

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.100
for self-service
application-centric monitoring

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.400
and diagnosis of database
performance issues.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.370
To access Insights, I
can click Query Insights

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.535
in the left navigation.

00:00:23.535 --> 00:00:24.910
I'm going to walk
you through how

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.210
to use Insights to identify and
diagnose a database performance

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.380
problem in an application.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.800
In this scenario, I'm
an application developer

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.980
on a team that developed a
ride-sharing application.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.870
We've gotten a report
from some of our users

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.330
that the application
is running slowly.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.130
Let's look at how
I can use Insights

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.930
to investigate and find
the cause of the problem.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.380
Looking at the top-level
query insights dashboard,

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.910
I'll start with
the database load

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.580
all queries graph, which
can help me visually

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.990
identify performance problems
or spikes in database load.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.090
The database load graph
confirms that there's

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.450
a problem that started
happening around 12 o'clock.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.040
The colors in the
graph help me see

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.400
that there's an increase in
CPU and an even larger increase

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.780
in lock weight.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.520
Now I need to find
out which query

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.820
is causing the problem, what the
root cause of the problem is,

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.090
and where in my application
code the problem lies.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:18.610
To try to identify which
query is causing the problem,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.770
I'll start with
the queries table.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.020
The queries table contains
normalized queries

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.850
for the time frame selected.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.910
The queries in the table are
sorted by total execution time

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:31.240
by default, but I can also
filter it by other criteria,

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.570
like CPU time, I/O
weight, or lock weight.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.190
The first query in
the table certainly

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.070
stands out, as its total
execution time is much larger

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.110
than the rest of the entries.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.420
Like many modern
applications, this application

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.658
consists of several
different services

00:01:45.658 --> 00:01:47.950
and I want to know which one
is causing the performance

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.060
problem.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.650
Traditional
monitoring tools only

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.620
provide a query-centric
view of performance.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.990
With Insights, I can toggle
from the queries table

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.420
to the tags table to
see a list of queries

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.500
tagged by business logic, giving
me an application-centric view.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.570
The tags table is
especially helpful to me

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.970
as a developer, since
this application was built

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.190
using Django's ORM
rather than by writing

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:10.900
the SQL queries myself.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.050
Insights uses SQL commenter,
an open source library

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:17.590
which provides instrumentation
and wrappers to augment SQL

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.750
from frameworks in ORMs.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.870
If your application is using
one of the supported ORMs,

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.840
the queries are automatically
tagged with little to no change

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.560
to your application code.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.490
So you're more easily able
to identify query load

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.610
and pinpoint where the problem
may lie in your application,

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.430
since you can see information
like actions, controllers,

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:37.480
and routes.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.300
The first tag query for the
payment charge controller

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.230
looks like it's the problem.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:45.100
Now that I've identified that
this is likely the slow query,

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.562
I want to find
out why it's slow.

00:02:47.562 --> 00:02:50.020
I'm going to click through to
see more details for this tag

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:50.860
query.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.800
Insights include sample
executed query plans, which

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.620
I can use to understand
why the query is slow.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.830
Query plans can be complex
and difficult to understand,

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.990
but Insights provides
visual query plans

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:04.420
that can help me easily
detect which part of the query

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.890
is slow.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.650
Each part of the query shows
the latency, rows returned,

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.780
and the costs for
that operation.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.820
In this query, an
update is happening.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.370
The modified table looks
to be contributing the most

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.400
to the latency because
of locking contention.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.100
Now that we've identified
the slow query and the cause,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.290
let's confirm the
source of the problem.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.020
Insights provides in
context visualization

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:29.350
of end-to-end tracing to help me
find which application code is

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.550
causing the problem.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.430
In addition to being able to
see the controller and route,

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.090
it can also help me understand
if a particular host or user is

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.350
the source of a problem.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.420
While I'm viewing the
trace visualization

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.820
from within Insights
in this example,

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:46.630
Insights complements your
existing APM and observability

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:47.770
tools.

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.470
Insights provides database
metrics and traces

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:53.080
through the Open
Standard, OpenTelemetry,

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.170
and Cloud monitoring
and Cloud trace APIs.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.150
This makes it easy to
do end-to-end tracing

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.290
in your existing tools.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:01.910
In this video,
we've looked at ways

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.910
you can use Cloud SQL insights
to help with diagnosing

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.550
and troubleshooting
application performance issues

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.980
all from a single intuitive UI.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.910
In the description
below the video,

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.870
you'll find links to a blog
post introducing insights

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.950
and the documentation for Cloud
SQL insights and SQL commenter.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:22.780
Or you can click on the Codelab
to try it out for yourself.

